Three rules land in the house prompt, so every agent wrapper picks them up on its next lock bump.

`defineAcronyms` expands an acronym, initialism, or coined shorthand at first use, or does not use it: "pressure stall information (PSI)", then PSI after. The exception is a closed list of sixteen forms, not a judgment call, and `packages/agent-prompt/default.nix` now fails any render carrying an all-caps token that is neither on the list nor expanded somewhere in the text. That gate found two counterexamples inside the prompt it was added to: bare `CDP` in the browser rule and bare `DAG` in the fork rule, both now expanded. A carve-out phrased as "forms a general programmer already knows" would have left both, because the calibration deciding what counts is the one that wrote them.

`firstPrinciples` reasons from the mechanism rather than from precedent: derive what must be true from how the system works, then check the established practice against that. Where a decision rests on what the surrounding code already does, name the constraint that produced the pattern and check it still holds. `redesignAtTheRoot` already covered judging the shape of a system once you are designing it; this covers the step before, where an answer is inherited from the nearest example instead of derived, which is how a wrong convention survives every review that only asks whether a change matches its neighbors.

`subagentVerification` moves checking out of the context that did the work: before calling a change done, spawn a fresh subagent to check it against the requirement, scaled to the change, since a one-line fix does not need one. The threshold is the point. `packages/agent/policy/hooks.nix` ships `alwaysOnReview = false` because an always-armed Stop gate turned a one-line fix into a four-agent fan-out, and an unconditional mandate in prose would have reinstated exactly that. The three existing delegation rules all described sending work outward and none described sending verification outward.

The review that caught both the unconditional mandate and the unenforceable carve-out was itself a subagent reading the diff in a context that had not written it.
